 ## Type Marshaling
 
-Shape values are converted to native types at the language boundary and back on return.
+Values cross as MessagePack, and the set of Shape types that have a projection
+is **closed**. It is one table for every language runtime: the host classifies
+the declared Shape type once, and each extension renders that classification
+into its own language. Neither side parses the other's type spellings.
+
+| Shape declared type | Python | TypeScript | Crosses |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `int` (also `i64`) | `int` | `ShapeInt` (an alias for `number`) | both ways |
+| `number` (also `float`, `f64`) | `float` | `number` | both ways |
+| `bool` | `bool` | `boolean` | both ways |
+| `string` (also `str`) | `str` | `string` | both ways |
+| `none` (also `()`) | `None` | `null` | both ways — but a `none` parameter carries nothing |
+| `Array<T>` / `Vec<T>`, `T` a scalar above | `list[T]` | `T[]` | both ways |
+| `HashMap<string, V>`, `V` a scalar other than `none` | `dict[str, V]` | `Record<string, V>` | **into the foreign function only** |
+| `Option<T>` / `T?` | `T` or `None` | `T` or `null` | wherever `T` does |
+| object type — a named `type`, or an inline `{ a: int }` | `TypedDict` | `interface` | see [Returning Structured Data](#returning-structured-data) |
+
+Two asymmetries are worth stating plainly, because they are not typos:
+
+- **`HashMap` goes in, not out.** There is an outbound projection and no inbound
+  one, so a map is a legal parameter and a rejected return type. Return an
+  object type instead — those project in both directions.
+- **Only scalars ride inside a container.** `Array<int>` crosses;
+  `Array<Measurement>` and `Array<Array<number>>` do not. Send the columns as
+  parallel scalar arrays, or pass one object per call.
+
+The return annotation may wrap the crossable type in exactly one `Result<...>`
+— that wrapper is the runtime's error channel and is stripped before the type
+is classified. A nested `Result`, or a `Result` in parameter position, has no
+projection.
+
