+Every rejection carries a fix hint naming a concrete rewrite that keeps the
+call. The distinct reasons a type lands here:
+
+| Reason | Example | What to do |
+|---|---|---|
+| No wire projection at all | `DataTable`, `DateTime`, `decimal`, `bigint`, `char`, `Set`, `Range`, `Content`, `ptr` | Pass it as a `string` (or `int` / `number` if it is numeric) and rebuild it in the foreign body |
+| Non-scalar element in a container | `Array<Measurement>`, `Array<Array<number>>`, `HashMap<string, Point>` | Parallel scalar arrays, or one object per call |
+| Map key that is not `string` | `HashMap<int, number>` | Declare `HashMap<string, V>` — MessagePack map keys cross as strings |
+| A constructor with no foreign form | tuples, function types, unions, `&T`, `dyn Trait` | Declare an object type with the fields you need |
+| Mapped, but not in this direction | `HashMap<string, int>` as a return type | Return an object type |
+| Wrong generic arity | `Option<A, B>` | Check the type arguments |
+
+Because function types have no foreign form, **closures and function objects
+cannot cross the language boundary** — that is one of the `[C0933]` cases, not
+a separate rule.
+
+The language server reports the same `[C0933]` at the same span, so the editor
+and `shape run` cannot disagree about whether a signature is expressible.
+
+`extern "C"` declarations are **not** covered by this table. They marshal
+through libffi against C types (`ptr`, `i32`, `cslice`, …), which is a
+different table with its own checks.
 
