+### Generated `.d.ts` Stubs
+
+On the first `fn typescript` link in a program, the host classifies the
+program's whole declared foreign contract and hands it to the extension, which
+renders a `.d.ts` document describing it:
+
+```typescript
+// Generated by the Shape TypeScript runtime extension — do not edit.
+// Interface declarations for the declared Shape contract (ADR-019 §1).
+
+/** Shape `int` (i64). Structurally a `number`: values beyond 2^53 lose
+ *  precision crossing the boundary. */
+type ShapeInt = number;
+
+interface Candle {
+  open: number;
+  label?: string;
+}
+
+declare function analyze(c: Candle): number;
+```
+
+#### `ShapeInt`
+
+Shape separates `int` (i64) from `number` (f64); TypeScript has a single
+numeric type. Rendering both as `number` would erase a distinction the
+declaration made and leave the stub unable to describe the contract it was
+generated from, so `int` renders as the emitted alias `ShapeInt` and `number`
+renders as `number`.
+
+The alias is structurally `number`, so `tsc` will not enforce integrality — it
+is there to keep the declaration readable and to put the range caveat where an
+author will see it. That caveat is real: an `int` beyond 2^53 cannot be
+represented exactly as a JavaScript number and loses precision crossing the
+boundary.
+
+The rest of the rendering follows the marshaling table: `bool` → `boolean`,
+`string` → `string`, `none` → `null` (MessagePack nil arrives as `null`, never
+`undefined`), `Array<T>` → `T[]`, `HashMap<string, V>` →
+`Record<string, V>`, and `Option<T>` → `T | null`.
+
+Two cases are worth knowing:
+
+- An **inline** object type gets a generated interface — `ShapeObject1`,
+  `ShapeObject2` — declared before the function that uses it, one interface per
+  distinct field shape however often it appears.
+- A type the host could **not** export field-by-field, because one of its
+  fields has a type with no wire form, is emitted with an
+  `[key: string]: unknown` index signature and a comment saying so. An empty
+  interface in TypeScript accepts anything, which would be a lie about the
+  contract.
+
+The contract payload is versioned, and a payload written by a host speaking a
+version the extension does not know is refused rather than guessed at: a
+misread contract yields a confidently wrong stub, which is worse than no stub.
+
+**The stub document is in memory only.** It lives on the VM that produced it
+(`VirtualMachine::foreign_stub_document("typescript")`); nothing writes a
+`.d.ts` to disk, and `tsserver` does not read one. On-disk materialization is
+POLY-FOREIGN-CHECK (**#197**).
+
